HOW TO RUN THIS DISK====================This disk is NOT a boot disk, and os9exec does not boot anything.  There isno kernel here and no OS9Boot file.  os9exec is the kernel: you point it at adisk with OS9DISK and name a program to run, and that disk is what OS-9 thensees as /dd -- its root and its home.  There is no established name for thatrole, which is why people reach for "boot disk"; this disk cannot bootanything.Many programs here carry hardcoded /h0 paths -- 70 want /h0/sys/termcap and50 want data of their own, like /h0/games/hack/playground.  That is notdamage: they were built by people whose freeware lived on /h0.  Arrange /h0and they all work.  The three arrangements below differ mainly in how theydo that.------------------------------------------------------------------1. YOU ALREADY RUN OS-9  --  recommended------------------------------------------------------------------Keep your own system as /dd and mount this collection as /h0:    OS9DISK=<your own disk>  OS9H0=<this image, named h0>  os9exec shellThen add this disk's commands to your path, in your own startup:    setenv PATH /dd/CMDS:/h0/CMDS:/h0/CMDS/GAMESPATH works in the OS-9 shell -- a bare command name is found along it, notonly in the execution directory.  (Verified: with /h0/CMDS on PATH a bare`rndname' runs; with it removed the same command gives E_PNNF.)This is the best arrangement, and not only for the paths:  - /dd stays yours -- your CMDS, your LIB, your startup, your cio.  - Because your cio is on /dd, the STARRED programs work too (see    DOC/README-CIO).  You get all of them, not just the 339 that need    nothing.  - Every hardcoded /h0 path resolves against this disk, where its data is.The image must be a file NAMED h0: os9exec resolves an image by filename, soOS9H0 pointing at a file called anything else fails with error 214.Two things to know:  - If /h0 is already YOUR disk, keep it that way and see arrangement 3.  - The image must stay writable.  hack, larn and cribbage write into    /h0/games/*/playground and criblog.------------------------------------------------------------------2. YOU HAVE NO OS-9 OF YOUR OWN------------------------------------------------------------------Run this disk by itself.  It carries public-domain shells, so no Microwaremodule is needed anywhere:    OS9DISK=<this image>  os9exec /dd/CMDS/bash_newFor the /h0 paths, give os9exec the image under a second name.  A hard linkcosts nothing -- one inode, two names, still 125 MB:    ln dd h0    OS9DISK=<...>/dd  OS9H0=<...>/h0  os9exec /dd/CMDS/bash_new(os9exec will not mount one path as two devices; the second name is whatmakes it work.  Hard links do not survive zip or plain tar, so make the linkafter unpacking.)The 339 unstarred programs all run this way.  The 87 starred ones need a cioyou do not have -- DOC/README-CIO lists free replacements for the ones thatmatter.Caveats when running the disk alone:  - Run programs by their FULL path (/dd/CMDS/cookie).  bash's PATH search    does not resolve against this filesystem, so a bare `cookie' says    "command not found" even with PATH set and the program sitting there.  - `sh', which the startup script names as its shell, starts but cannot    fork ANY external command.  It sees the file, agrees it is executable,    and the fork still fails.  Use bash_new.  - `ksh' is the nicest of the three but wants cio loaded before it starts,    which needs a shell first -- so it cannot be the first shell.------------------------------------------------------------------3. TAKE ONLY WHAT YOU WANT------------------------------------------------------------------Nobody needs SNOBOL, FORTRAN and XLISP at once.  To move a few programs ontoyour own disk and keep your own /h0:  - Copy this disk's GAMES/ to your own disk, so /h0/games/... resolves    against yours.  That is what the game data paths expect.  - Read DOC/DEPENDS before copying any single program.  It lists, per    program, every absolute path that program carries -- generated by    scanning the binaries.  110 of them need something besides their own    binary: a dictionary, a skeleton file, a scratch directory.  Copying    just the command often is not enough.  - Your own /h0/sys/termcap is then the one that gets used, which is    probably better tuned for your terminal than ours.DOC/INDEX says what each program is; DOC/ORIGINS says where it came from;SOURCES.txt records the terms it arrived under.